#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:5
#: 59acc5f4081d4b66aa4252b6ac4b9726
msgid "Mocking an Airspeed Sensor for Bench Testing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:7
#: 479aaf1bdf994190af2ac37890cc4891
msgid "This article explains how you can use a potentiometer with an autopilot in order to simulate the input from an Airspeed Sensor. This is very useful for ground testing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:12
#: 5ca8683b40294f4a870b58c185d1e1e3
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:14
#: 155ee516b00549bd8c04c17a79958eba
msgid "If your aerodynamic surfaces (i.e. auto flaps, ailerons, elevators, elevons, etc.) are geared or actuated with airspeed, it may be helpful to ground test them on your aircraft by sending the autopilot a specific airspeed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:19
#: c0c844019c8e45d28669d0f3165e9a25
msgid "To do this you must apply a voltage to an analog input pin designated as the analog airspeed input. On a Pixhawk you can plug a potentiometer into ADC 6.6v (pin 15) or ADC 3.3v (pin 11). If you are using an I2C :ref:`airpseed <airspeed>` sensor you will temporarily configure your autopilot to use the analogue port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:33
#: 98df8464ed264d53bf9f40b9479c571d
msgid "Required parts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:35
#: 080e9957632843e5a7c48fef4b7f74fe
msgid "You will need a DF-13 cable and a simple `potentiometer like this <https://www.sparkfun.com/products/9939>`__ or `this <http://hobbyking.com/hobbyking/store/__39510__Potentiameter_Round_Pot_Turnigy_9XR_Transmitter_3set_.html>`__. Either a B10K and B5K potentiometer can be used to set speed from 0 to as much as 90 m/s."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:39
#: 19a4ed4e8d3a49baae4c7932c6e103ce
msgid "Pixhawk Configuration and Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:41
#: d59a59e672f345b69926845ca90d0e72
msgid "Once the DF 13 cable is connected to the potentiometer, plug it into the :ref:`ADC 6.6v port <common-pixhawk-overview_pixhawk_analog_input_pins>`. Open your Ground Control Station (GCS) of choice, and change the ``ARSPD_PIN`` parameter to Pin \"**15\"** as shown below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:49
#: 25dde5b2725341329f89775946b928f7
msgid "Reboot your Pixhawk to allow this parameter change to have an effect. Airspeed will reset to 1-3 m/s after the restart and initialization is complete. Adjust the pot to set your desired speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/mocking-an-airspeed-sensor-for-bench-testing.rst:55
#: c1736d87665e4aba98a40ced4e1dab98
msgid "If you are using an I2C airspeed sensor - *remember to re-enable it!* Set the ``ARSPD_PIN`` parameter to 65, and cycle the autopilot power to ensure the I2C airspeed properly initializes. Check you get the normal 1-3 m/s noise on the airspeed display."
msgstr ""
